# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Credentials and local tool configuration
#
# Added after a `git add -A` swept opencode.json into a commit. That file holds
# a live GitHub personal access token, and GitHub push protection rejected the
# push - the only reason it never reached origin/main. It was a lucky outcome:
# nothing in the local gates would have caught it, because a leaked token is not
# a syntax error and not a type error.
#
# The rule generalised: `git add -A` in a working tree where tool configs appear
# has an unbounded blast radius, and the mitigation is to make the credential
# shapes un-addable rather than to remember not to add them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Tool configuration that carries credentials. opencode.json holds
# GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN; .mcp.json is the other standard MCP location and
# has the same shape.
opencode.json
.mcp.json

# Environment files, matched at any depth rather than only at the repository
# root. A first attempt anchored these with a leading slash so that
# `.env.example` would survive by accident; a negative control then showed the
# cost - a token in `agent/.env` was unprotected, which is just as leaked as one
# at the root. So the pattern is broad and the example forms are re-admitted
# explicitly, which is the trade the previous version was making by accident.
.env*
!.env.example
!.env.sample
!.env.template

# Cluster credentials. The E2E workflow reads KUBECONFIG from the environment,
# so a developer's kubeconfig landing at the repo root is an expected accident.
kubeconfig
kubeconfig.yaml
*.kubeconfig

# Key material. `*.key` is narrow: this repository has no tracked file matching
# it today, which was checked rather than assumed, and a `Secret` fixture named
# `something.key` would be a manifest test rather than key material.
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
id_rsa
id_ed25519

# Cloud and package credentials, which follow developers into a repo root by
# habit rather than by intent.
.aws/
.gcloud/
credentials.json
service-account*.json
.netrc
.npmrc
.pypirc

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Kubernetes Secret manifests
#
# Deliberately narrow, and the tension is real: a redacted Secret fixture is a
# legitimate thing to commit and blocking it would be wrong, but an unredacted
# one is the single most common leak in a Kubernetes repository. Scoped to the
# repository root so `deploy/**` can still hold reviewed, redacted fixtures,
# which is what the deploy tests read.
#
# Nothing tracked matches this today.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/secrets.yaml
/secrets.yml

# Tooling binaries dropped into the working tree rather than installed.
# github-mcp-server.exe is also matched by `*.exe` above; it is named explicitly
# because it is unexpected in a Go/Python repository and should be recognised
# when someone finds it.
/github-mcp-server